WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
Speaker 1: OK, let's get into audience questions&nbsp;
first one very timely, very important, David,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:10.560
the federal eviction moratorium is ending&nbsp;
soon. What is going to happen when all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:16.720
these people get evicted in the next few months?&nbsp;
Well, that's that's not going to happen, actually.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:22.400
I think it's important to. Let's contextualize&nbsp;
it. Yes. I believe July 31st is actually the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.400
day that the federal eviction moratorium&nbsp;
that's covid related will come to an end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.120
But there's a bunch of different reasons&nbsp;
which explain why you are not going to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:38.720
nearly even a majority of the people currently in&nbsp;
theory in at risk of getting evicted, get evicted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:44.000
Dean Baker, the economist, talked to us about this&nbsp;
last week. He said the number being bandied about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.400
is about eight million people could get evicted,&nbsp;
but that he believes in reality it will be more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:53.840
like 800000. And as he said, it doesn't mean&nbsp;
we don't care about the eight hundred thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:01.520
but eight hundred thousand evictions nationally&nbsp;
is not necessarily going to be a major economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.200
disaster. Doesn't mean we don't care about the&nbsp;
people, doesn't mean we don't need to fix the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:10.080
situation. So there's a few different things that&nbsp;
you have to remember. First of all, depending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:16.400
on what state or even city you live in, the fact&nbsp;
that the federal moratorium guidance from the CDC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:23.680
ends on July 31st doesn't necessarily impact&nbsp;
you because there are states and in some cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.720
even cities that are choosing different dates.&nbsp;
So there are already some states with moratoria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:33.760
or moratoriums colloquially that&nbsp;
are in place that will go longer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:39.120
And two of the states or some of the states&nbsp;
with the largest populations like California,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:46.160
Texas and New York, have protections that go&nbsp;
beyond July 31st already both for residential as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:52.880
well as for business evictions. You've got other&nbsp;
states like Illinois, Oregon, Hawaii, New Jersey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:59.680
Washington, New Mexico. They have their own&nbsp;
guidelines in place that go longer than July 31st.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:07.440
Texas has local eviction moratorium that has been&nbsp;
extended to, I believe it's October 1st or maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:15.360
October 31st. So there's the idea that on a single&nbsp;
day all these people are going to get evicted. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:20.320
reality is many states have already extended that.&nbsp;
The other reality and this doesn't help people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:27.040
it's just from an economic shock perspective,&nbsp;
it's important. It takes a long time to evict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:32.000
people and it takes a different amount of time&nbsp;
in different municipalities. It can take months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:41.120
and months to evict people. I know various lawyers&nbsp;
that work on evictions, both with tenants and with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.480
landlords. Often it's the same lawyers&nbsp;
that work with both, not in the same case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:49.040
but work with both. They say depending on the&nbsp;
state, you could take three to even six months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.560
depending on if it's a place where it gets cold&nbsp;
in the winter. There are even bigger restrictions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:57.120
in place all the time. So there's not going&nbsp;
to be a single day of evictions. Now, one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:03.600
additional factor here, California and&nbsp;
New York have announced programs where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:09.440
they are going to pay past due rent for a lot&nbsp;
of people who are at risk of getting evicted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:16.880
So in those places, when the moratoriums finally&nbsp;
do end, there aren't going to be mass evictions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:24.160
because the states have put in place these&nbsp;
programs. So for me, the bigger question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:31.520
is the following. So many people live paycheck&nbsp;
to paycheck. You miss one paycheck, you miss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:37.600
a payment for your rent. You can essentially&nbsp;
never catch up. There's an emergency. You've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:41.840
got a health insurance plan with a two thousand&nbsp;
dollar deductible. And something happens where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.840
you've got to pay the two thousand dollars&nbsp;
all at once. And so you miss a rent payment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.320
You essentially can never catch up.&nbsp;
And that is a bigger reality that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.920
whatever you feel about paying past due rent, like&nbsp;
California, for example, put in place doing, even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:03.040
if you feel like that's the right thing to do or&nbsp;
whatever, even getting a lot of people out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:08.000
current situation. Imagine someone who's behind on&nbsp;
rent because of the pandemic, lost their job. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:14.080
they're kind of keeping up, but they're not able&nbsp;
to catch up. They're going to end up statistically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:17.600
anyway. Some percentage of them are going to end&nbsp;
up back in that situation anyway because so many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:22.320
people are living paycheck to paycheck. That's not&nbsp;
going to change, even with rent forgiveness. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:28.240
last thought on this, there are people who say&nbsp;
that because real estate prices have become so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:35.360
overblown in many parts of the country, that an&nbsp;
eviction and even a foreclosure crisis would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:43.600
a really good thing. It would pull down prices.&nbsp;
And that is not necessarily wrong in some states.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:50.320
But there is a question as to whether we should&nbsp;
hope for the misery and stress and anxiety of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:57.040
being evicted as a path towards a modest reduction&nbsp;
in housing prices, which may or may not even be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.000
permanent. So that's kind of a bigger discussion,&nbsp;
but that's the bottom line on evictions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:06.880
they're not going to happen all at once, not that&nbsp;
many are actually going to take place. And you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:10.720
got to really pay attention to what's happening in&nbsp;
your state and in your municipality in particular.

